(* Kaguya, stage 1. Quoted items are terminals: tokens the lexer emits, written
   with the same text. ident and number are terminals too; how they are spelled
   is lexer.py's rule. NL is the endline token. *)

program    ::= { statement } exit_stmt

statement  ::= decl | assign | if | while

decl       ::= type [ "🔓" ] ident "⟨" expr "⟩"

type       ::= "3⃣" | "6⃣" | "❓"

assign     ::= ident ":=" expr

if         ::= "🤔" expr NL block [ "🙃" NL block ]

while      ::= "🔁" expr NL block

block      ::= "{" NL { statement } [ exit_stmt NL ] "}" NL

exit_stmt  ::= "🚪" expr

expr       ::= arith [ ( "==" | "≠" ) arith ]

arith      ::= term { ( "+" | "-" ) term }

term       ::= factor { "*" factor }

factor     ::= number
             | "✅"
             | "❌"
             | ident
             | "(" expr ")"
             | "!" factor
             | "-" factor


(* Line structure is lexical: the lexer gives one token vector per line, the
   parser drops the endline tokens, and a line with no tokens is skipped. So
   one statement per line and blank lines cost no rule here.

   program is what rejects a statement after exit and a program without one:
   exit_stmt sits outside the repetition, so it is required and it is last.
   A block says the same thing one level down, which is why NL is written out:
   a statement used to be a line, but an if is several, so the parser needs a
   line cursor beside its token cursor and the grammar needs to say where the
   line ends. '{', '}' and '🙃' each stand alone on theirs.

   '⟨' '⟩' delimit an initialiser and '{' '}' delimit a block, and neither does
   the other's job. The parser needs no lookahead to tell them apart, and a
   brace on the '🤔' line is an ordinary unexpected-token error rather than a
   special case.

   Mutability is const by default. '🔓' is optional and follows the type, so a
   decl line always starts with its type token and parse_statement dispatches
   on one token.

   expr allows at most one comparison, and it binds weaker than arithmetic: the
   second arith is the only place "==" or "≠" can appear, so a second
   comparison is left over after the expression and rejected there.

   '!' and unary '-' are in factor and recurse on factor, so they bind tighter
   than every binary operator: !a == b compares !a with b, -a * b is (-a) * b,
   and !!a is a factor twice over. '(' expr ')' is the only way to override the
   precedence that arith and term fix.

   exit_stmt takes a full expr, not a factor: '🚪 a + 1' and '🚪 (x == y)' parse.

   Types are not in the grammar: 3⃣ a ⟨ ✅ ⟩ parses. Left out on purpose, checked
   in the semantic pass of stage 2: declared before use, declared once, 🔓
   before :=, and the type rules. A bool condition and a bool under '!' are two
   more of the same kind, and so is every overflow check.

   Scopes are not in the grammar either. A block is one rule here and one frame
   in the semantic pass and one builder position in codegen, and those three do
   not line up: merge belongs to no brace, and an exit ends a basic block
   without popping a frame. *)
